\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Republic of Iceland Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2018\par 22:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 8 Jun 2018 16:08 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Republic of Iceland Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We expect the currently overheated Icelandic economy to gradually cool \par down over the next few years. The current account balance will weaken as \par tourism sector growth moderates amid the Icelandic krona's rapid appreciation. \par      -- At the same time, we forecast continued strong fiscal performance with \par net general government debt stabilizing at 30% of GDP over the next four years. \par      -- We are affirming our 'A/A-1' ratings on Iceland. \par      -- The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On June 8, 2018, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its long- and short-term foreign \par and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on the Republic of Iceland at \par 'A/A-1'. The outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook balances the risks stemming from the domestic economy \par overheating against the potential for more rapid improvements in the \par government and external balance sheets over the next few years. \par  \par We could raise the ratings on Iceland if its external and fiscal performance \par were to significantly exceed our current expectations, improving its public \par and external balance sheet well beyond what we currently anticipate. \par  \par We could lower the ratings on Iceland if economic overheating were place at \par risk its long-term growth prospects, balance of payments performance, or \par financial stability. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The ratings on Iceland reflect the country's strong institutional framework \par and general policymaking effectiveness, comparatively low net general \par government debt at 30% of GDP, and high per capita income levels. The ratings \par are constrained by Iceland's vulnerability to external developments as well as \par typically high volatility throughout the economic cycle. The ratings are also \par constrained by risks stemming from the domestic economy overheating. \par  \par Institutional and Economic Profile: The overheating of the domestic economy \par poses risks, but so far we project a soft landing \par      -- GDP growth will moderate to 2%-3% in 2018-2021, cooling off from a \par brisk expansion. \par      -- Signs of overheating are showing in the domestic economy and could \par pose risks. \par      -- Still, with GDP per capita of over US$70,000, Iceland's income levels \par are among the highest globally, while the economy also benefits from high \par education standards and favorable demographics. \par  \par The economy of Iceland expanded by 3.6% in real terms in 2017, which is a \par notable slowdown compared to 7.5% real GDP growth in 2016. We project growth \par will further moderate to 2.8% in 2018. This will be partly driven by a \par slowdown in the pace of tourism sector expansion but also by the completion of \par some investment projects, for instance in the metals sector. This should help \par cool the recent economic overheating, which has been particularly evident in \par recent exchange rate, labor, and housing market trends. \par  \par That said, we continue to see multiple risks. \par  \par Several rounds of wage negotiations are coming up in 2018 and early 2019. \par Iceland has a history of wage increases notably exceeding productivity growth \par and inflation. Given the recent strong economic performance, we believe \par certain labor market groups could demand substantial wage hikes, which may \par have country-wide implications if other groups follow on. In our view, this \par poses risks to the competitiveness of the domestic economy. \par  \par Competitiveness risks are also stemming from the strengthening of the \par Icelandic krona exchange rate, which has appreciated by over 20% in real \par effective terms (CPI-based) over the last three years. The tourism sector \par underpinned much of Iceland's recent economic expansion, posting double-digit \par growth rates in 2012-2016. It has also contributed significantly to the \par strengthening of Iceland's balance of payments position, ultimately \par facilitating the resolution of the old overhang and the removal of capital \par controls in March 2017. However, the rapid expansion has also been a key \par factor behind the aforementioned strengthening of the exchange rate. Together \par with wage hikes, this presents competitiveness challenges for the domestic \par economy. This is particularly the case for sectors with predominantly \par krona-denominated costs, such as IT, and in the long-run could undermine \par Iceland's diversification prospects. Positively, these risks are somewhat \par mitigated by the country's flexible labor markets: labor importation has \par picked up substantially in recent years, which at least partially alleviates \par the impact of rising domestic costs. \par  \par During the first few months of 2018, there have been signs of tourist inflows \par moderating. This likely stems from Iceland becoming an increasingly expensive \par destination as well as indicating capacity constraints. In our view, the \par moderation of inflows could actually prove positive for the broader economy as \par it reduces the overheating risks. We assume that tourist inflows will \par demonstrate a flat dynamic or only a slow growth over the next three years. As \par a result, we expect the exchange rate to weaken by 13% through 2021. \par  \par However, if tourism inflows were to abruptly decrease rather than flatten out, \par this could expose multiple vulnerabilities. Specifically, we believe that the \par housing market could weaken with knock-on effects on household and banks' \par balance sheets. \par  \par More broadly, we note that Iceland's economy remains small in absolute terms \par with GDP of US$24 billion in 2017. It is structurally volatile and ultimately \par dependent on the economic performance of main trading partners and \par developments in terms of trade, which have been particularly favorable over \par the last few years. Even so, at US$71,000 in 2017, Iceland's GDP per capita is \par among the highest globally, which we view as a key strength. This is because \par high income levels indirectly indicate potentially higher resilience to \par withstand economic shocks, as well as a broader base that could theoretically \par be taxed. \par  \par We believe the country also benefits from high levels of education, favorable \par demographics, and the presence of natural resources, such as very competitive \par hydro and thermo energy production. These bode well for longer-term prospects. \par  \par Importantly, we view Iceland's institutional arrangements as another rating \par strength. There are broadly functioning checks and balances between various \par state bodies and consensus on key issues, such as the need to maintain a \par comparatively prudent fiscal position. Iceland's coalition government \par following the October 2017 snap elections comprises three parties spanning the \par left and right of the political spectrum. The coalition commands a narrow \par majority in parliament and, as such, we believe it could be more challenging \par to agree on some specific policy items. However, we do not expect a major \par shift in policy direction compared to recent years. \par  \par Flexibility and Performance Profile: Current account is set to weaken but \par fiscal and external buffers remain \par      -- We forecast the current account to swing into deficit by 2020. \par      -- Spending pressures remain but we project only modest fiscal deficits \par from 2019. \par      -- Monetary policy flexibility has improved following the earlier lifting \par of capital controls but remains structurally constrained by the economy's size \par and significant dependence on foreign developments. \par  \par We project Iceland's current account balance to stand at 1.3% in 2018 and \par swing to a slight deficit by 2020. This is mainly driven by our projection of \par tourism receipts leveling off and strong consumption pulling in imports. \par Strong growth in the tourism sector has underpinned a substantial improvement \par in Iceland's external position in recent years. We estimate that the economy \par is currently in a small net external asset position. While recent years' \par external performance has built up buffers against the reemergence of external \par financing pressures, a sudden stop of tourism revenues could pose a \par significant threat to Iceland's balance of payments. We expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central \par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Iceland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  's (CBI) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and hence \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve \par growth to be rather limited, but we anticipate a pick-up in foreign investment \par by the Icelandic pension funds. They have had limited opportunities previously \par having spent years operating within the confines of capital controls. Overall, \par we forecast our preferred measure of net external indebtedness relative to \par current account receipts rising to around 53% by 2021. \par  \par On the back of the economic recovery, Iceland's fiscal performance has also \par improved, reaching a 1.5% general government surplus in 2017. We project \par modest deficits from 2019 onward. This is based on the government's fiscal \par agenda, which includes infrastructure and health care investments, increased \par spending on social services, and some loosening of tax policy with only \par limited revenue-raising measures. We believe further spending pressures could \par emerge, including for political reasons, given the only narrow majority \par commanded by the current administration. Combined with our more conservative \par growth projections, this underpins our fiscal deficit forecasts from 2019 \par while the government currently projects the general government sector to post \par recurrent surpluses in the medium term, as outlined in a recent fiscal policy \par statement. Given our deficit projections, we forecast net general government \par debt to stabilize at close to 30% of GDP. \par  \par There is potential, however, for the pace of debt reduction to be faster than \par we presently anticipate. This could happen, for example, if the authorities go \par ahead with the sale of commercial banks and use the proceeds to pay down debt \par (which we have not factored into our base-case scenario). It could also happen \par if the fiscal policy is tightened more in an attempt to cool off the economic \par boom, but that so far seems difficult given the composition of the governing \par coalition. \par  \par We still see some risks from contingent fiscal liabilities--particularly from \par the high stock of government guarantees to public-sector entities, such as the \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8 Jun 2018 16:08 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Republic of Iceland -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Housing Financing Fund or energy company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Landsvirkjun\f28 \uc2 \f2  --which we currently \par assess as moderate. \par  \par In our view, Iceland's monetary flexibility has improved following the March \par 2017 lifting of capital controls and given the adherence to a broadly flexible \par exchange rate arrangement since then. We also believe that the accumulation of \par substantial extra net \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves has underpinned the CBI's \par improved ability to act as a lender of last resort for the domestic banking \par system both in foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Nevertheless, policy \par effectiveness remains limited by the structural features of the Icelandic \par economy, including its small size and the inflation level's pronounced \par dependence on the value of the exchange rate and foreign prices. \par  \par We also note that Iceland's real effective exchange rate (REER) tends to be \par particularly volatile over the economic cycle, and subject to large valuation \par swings that have historically exacerbated the cyclicality already inherent in \par Iceland's economy. According to CBI data, the consumer price index-based REER \par has appreciated steeply in recent years (by 30% since end-2014) and now hovers \par at the level of the pre-2008 peak. The REER calculated on the basis of unit \par labor cost has appreciated by an even larger 40% over the same period. \par Inflation has only recently, in first-quarter 2018, reached the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \par 2.5% target after three years of hovering slightly below 2.0%. We expect \par inflation to slightly accelerate to 3.0% in 2018-2019 before moderating again \par to 2.5% thereafter. \par  \par The Icelandic banks remain well-capitalized and liquid. Domestic credit has \par picked up lately, and we expect it to grow roughly in line with nominal GDP \par over the next three years. This is a notable turnaround as domestic credit \par growth has been negative until 2015. Importantly, substantial house price \par hikes, particularly in the capital area, have therefore not been fueled by the \par financial system funding to the domestic economy. In fact, private sector \par leverage has been on a steady declining trend as a percentage of GDP since \par 2008 and has only recently shown signs of picking up. \par  \par In our view, there are risks stemming from the increasing role pension funds \par are playing in providing domestic credit. Over the last few years, they have \par been increasing their share of the market at the expense of commercial banks. \par The market share in total lending to households has risen to 18%, from 10%, \par over the last two years. We see risks stemming from this development as the \par pension funds' supervision and their capacity to monitor credit risks is \par untested. We understand that there is a built-in adjustment mechanism in that \par the poor performance of a pension fund could ultimately lead to a lower \par pension payout. However, this could then affect the financial position of \par households and may also have fiscal implications. \par  \par For more information on the banking system, see "Banking Industry Country Risk \par Assessment: Iceland," published Feb. 21, 2018. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, June 5, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, June 5, 2018 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: First-Quarter 2018, April 11, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, April 10, 2018. An interactive version is \par also available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2018: