\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Markets-Stocks bounce, IMF in focus\b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2008\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Emerging equities bounce 5 percent, Russia up 7 pct\par \par \uc2 * Benchmark Turkey dollar bond rises more than 8 points\par \par \uc2 * Several countries exploring possibility of help from IMF\par \par \uc2 * Credit default swaps ease as risk aversion subsides\par \par \uc2 By Carolyn Cohn\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Emerging markets bounced 5 percent on Tuesday, adding to gains of over 7 percent made in the previous session, as markets welcomed global plans for bank bail-outs, but some economies were exploring the idea of IMF support. The United States is due to unveil a plan at 1230 GMT to inject $250 billion into its banks, following similar, concerted measures in Europe in the past few days.\par \par \uc2 Emerging stocks shot up, tracking global moves, while Turkey's benchmark sovereign dollar bond <TRGLB30=RR> rose more than 8 points.\par \par \uc2 "The credit and money markets are finally responding favourably to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Akber Khan, director of CEEMA and LatAm equity focus at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This rally in equities looks like it has further to go at least in the short term -- the violent rally we've just seen only erases 4-5 days worth of losses."\par \par \uc2 Benchmark emerging equities <.MSCIEF> were trading up 5.2 percent at 1010 GMT, at 8-day highs. Russia's largest exchange MICEX <.MCX> was up over 11 percent prompting a one-hour trading suspension, while its dollar-denominated RTS index <.IRTS> rose nearly 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 However, emerging markets are starting to look to the possibility of International Monetary Fund support as some economies continue to look vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine plans to request an IMF funding programme, a senior IMF official said on Monday. [ID:nN13412076]\par \par \uc2 Iceland is also said to have requested financing from the IMF, although the IMF said it had not received an official request for help. [ID:nN13479740] Icelandic officials are discussing a loan to help the island's collapsing financial sector with Russia's Finance Ministry on Tuesday in talks that are expected to last until Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The IMF also said on Monday it stood ready to provide technical and financial report for Hungary, and Serbia said it would ask the IMF for a new deal.\par \par \uc2 "The focus is likely to shift to determining which countries are not in a position to be able to support their banking systems," said analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in a client note.\par \par \uc2 "The next phase for emerging markets could see the IMF becoming a far more active lender -- with US$200 billion in capital and a further US$50 billion in credit lines, the IMF stands in a strong position from which to provide precautionary funding to a range of countries."\par \par \uc2 Credit default swaps (CDS), used to insure against restructuring or default of debt, eased on Tuesday as investors took a slightly more sanguine approach to risk.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's five-year CDS narrowed to 1,300-1,750, one broker said, after trading above 1,500 on Monday, helped by Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves on Monday to shore up banking stability. [nLD311544]. Kazakhstan's CDS narrowed by 250 bps to 750-850, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered to sell dollars on Tuesday on the interbank market at 5.0 hryvnias <UAH=>. The hryvnia was trading at 5.07, up 1.58 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 The forint was up slightly against the euro <EURHUF=>, having recovered over 8 percent from two-year lows set on Friday.\par \par \uc2 But failing liquidity continued to hit emerging markets. There has been no real trade in the Icelandic crown <EURISK=> since Oct 8 and the Serbian dinar hit three-month lows against the euro <EURRSD=> despite heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Iceland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market <.OMXI15> fell 76 percent on Tuesday in its first trading since Oct 8, excluding six financial stocks.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Sebastian Tong; Editing by Victoria Main)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081017e4ae000xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
