\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Iceland crown surges on news of Russian loan\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2008\par 09:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with detail, background)\par \par \uc2 REYKJAVIK, Oct 7 (Reuters) - Iceland's crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged after news on Tuesday that Russia would give the country 4 billion euros in loans to help it through a financial crisis that threatened to overwhelm the national economy.\par \par \uc2 The Icelandic crown had dropped 35 percent earlier <EURISK=D3> on the news the state was taking over the country's second-biggest bank. But it staged a huge recovery to 220 per euro after Iceland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement that Russia had agreed to lend the money.\par \par \uc2 Using emergency powers adopted on Monday, Iceland dismissed the board of directors of \uc2 Landsbanki\uc2  and put the bank in receivership, a minister told state radio on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Commerce and banking minister Bjorgvin Sigurdsson said the bank would be open and run as normal while the changes were taking place. Iceland's Financial Supervisory Authority had put its own people in place of the bank's board.\par \par \uc2 Threatened with national bankruptcy, Iceland adopted sweeping powers over banks late on Monday as its financial system tottered and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 The ruling alliance and opposition parties united on a bill that gave the state the ability to dictate banking \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including provisions that allow it to push through mergers or even force a bank to declare bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 Parliament passed the bill and its provisions took effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 Investment firm Exista kicked off on Tuesday what is expected to be a string of Icelandic asset sales, saying it will sell its near 20 percent stake in Finnish insurer Sampo to reduce liabilities.\par \par \uc2 But Straumur-Burdaras , another large Icelandic financial firm, said it saw no sign that it needed state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kaupthing, the island's biggest bank, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Iceland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lent it 500 million euros. It said it had not been approached by the state's financial regulator.\par \par \uc2 SMALL NATION, BIG PROBLEMS\par \par \uc2 Iceland has found itself perched on a faultline in the global financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 "We were faced with the real possibility that the national economy would be sucked into the global banking swell and end in national bankruptcy," Prime Minister Geir Haarde told the nation late on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Iceland is an island in the middle of the North Atlantic, home to just 300,000 people.\par \par \uc2 But its financial significance has swelled in recent years as its banks expanded overseas, investors took large positions in its high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign firms poured money into local projects. (Reporting by Omar Valdimarsson via Stockholm Newsroom, writing by Anna Ringstrom and Adam Cox; Editing by Mark Trevelyan, Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 ICELAND-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 1, PIX TV)|LANGEN|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|ABN|J|Z|NAT|UKI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081007e4a7000fr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
