\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 9-Iceland adopts sweeping bank powers to stem crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Omar Valdimarsson   \par \uc2 874 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2008\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds parliament passes bill)\par \par \uc2  REYKJAVIK, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Threatened with national bankruptcy, Iceland agreed to adopt sweeping powers over banks on Monday as its financial system tottered, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged 30 percent and a leading agency cut its credit ratings.\par \par \uc2  The ruling alliance and opposition parties united on a bill that gave the state the ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at will in the country's battered banks. Parliament passed the bill and its provisions took effect immediately.\par \par \uc2  "We were faced with the real possibility that the national economy would be sucked into the global banking swell and end in national bankruptcy," said Prime Minister Geir Haarde.\par \par \uc2  In an address to the nation, he said Iceland's top financial regulator would have wide-ranging authority to dictate a bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and could even force it to merge with another firm or declare bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2  Standard and Poor's agency downgraded Iceland's credit rating by two notches to "BBB" from "A-", saying the authorities' actions would help to limit the risks to the budget but would also cut  banks' access to international markets, causing the economy to contract much more sharply than foreseen.\par \par \uc2  "This economic contraction, combined with the depreciation of the krona, will hurt the asset quality of the banks, depress the fiscal revenues of the state and markedly alter what had been low government debt levels," it said.\par \par \uc2  Iceland is an island in the middle of the North Atlantic, home to just 300,000 people. But it has found itself on the fault line of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2  The country's financial stature had swelled in recent years as its banks expanded overseas, investors took large positions in its high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign firms poured money into local projects.\par \par \uc2  But by Monday, the prime minister raised the spectre of a complete financial collapse had the bill not been agreed.\par \par \uc2  "The legislation is necessary to avoid that fate," Haarde said.\par \par \uc2  The bill will also allow the government to take over housing loans held by the banks and put them in a government housing fund, an effort to help thousands of islanders who faced the loss of their homes amid an ever-widening credit crunch.\par \par \uc2  BORROWING SPREE\par \par \uc2  The government held marathon talks through the weekend, with Haarde holding out hope such drastic measures would not be needed.\par \par \uc2  "As recently as last night, it looked like the banks could continue \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while," Haarde said. "This morning and today, things have totally changed for the worse."\par \par \uc2  The government ordered a halt in trading in all of the island's banks before the stock exchange opened on Monday.\par \par \uc2  But as the crown collapsed, the situation for Icelandic banks became dire. During the boom years that came to an abrupt halt late last year, Iceland's banks took on massive foreign debts as they expanded overseas.\par \par \uc2  The crown's fall means those debts have now become much more expensive to finance.\par \par \uc2  The crown, a volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the best of the times, began a descent on Monday that made its previous slides pale in comparison.\par \par \uc2  By late Monday, it had shed some 30 percent on the day to a record low of close to 230 per euro. In the wake of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall, inflation in the country, which is heavily dependent on imports, is set to rocket.\par \par \uc2  Haarde said it was hoped the banking system would not collapse but that was not yet certain. "It is possible that some banks may not be able to function without our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2  Haarde and an official at \b \highlight22\uc2 Iceland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the bill did not contain any provisions for a loan to Iceland's biggest bank, Kaupthing .\par \par \uc2  This had followed confusion as an early version of the bill, posted briefly on the Internet, was said to contain language that spoke of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan to Kaupthing. A later version of the bill did not contain the language.\par \par \uc2  Kaupthing had said on Sunday it was healthy. But markets were wary as authorities and bankers for months had said the financial sector was sound, and yet only last week Iceland's then-number three bank, Glitnir , had to be rescued.\par \par \uc2  Part of Iceland's plan has been to persuade pension funds to repatriate cash. Local news media reported over the weekend that the Icelandic Pension Funds Association had agreed to the idea, with pension funds being asked to transfer 200 billion Icelandic crowns ($1.8 billion) to the state.\par \par \uc2  The pension fund association issued a statement on Monday confirming it had encouraged its members to bring back capital. It said total assets of the funds amounted to 1.8 trillion Icelandic crowns, with about 500 billion in foreign securities.\par \par \uc2  The association has asked members to sell up to 50 percent of their foreign securities.  (Reporting by Omar Valdimarsson via Stockholm Newsroom, writing by Anna Ringstrom and Adam Cox; Editing by Jonathan Oatis and Mark Trevelyan)   \par \par \uc2 ICELAND-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 9, PIX TV)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA