\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Deutsche rates Icelandic bank debt short-term sell  \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail)  \par \par \uc2 STOCKHOLM, April 7 (Reuters) - Deutsche Bank said on Friday it was restarting coverage of Icelandic banks Kaupthing , Landsbanki and Glitnir with a short-term sell recommendation on their senior debt.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche said the banks' debt could have long-term support if the Icelandic economy deteriorates, because the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, equity exposure and hesitancy among foreign investors "could eventually have serious implications for the banks".  \par \par \uc2 "In our view, the Icelandic banks are exposed to substantial risk, and in our view the macroeconomic outlook is sufficiently gloomy for us to take a negative view on the next 6-9 months," said Deutsche.  \par \par \uc2 It was the latest warning that the Icelandic economy and its highly-geared banks, which have funded the aggressive expansion abroad of Icelandic companies, could face a crisis.  \par \par \uc2 The Icelandic crown <ISK=><EURISK=> has dropped 12 percent against the euro this year and 15 percent against the dollar on warnings from ratings agencies and banks that the boom is over.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key policy rate recently for the 13th time in under two years, tightening it by 75 basis points to 11.5 percent to rein in inflation pumped up by booming domestic demand, investment in smelters and the weak crown.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche said further depreciation could put more pressure on the economy while the banks could face difficulty meeting funding needs in 2007 with the cost of financing rising.  \par \par \uc2 With Iceland holding a negative foreign debt position, the banks could be affected if further depreciation had "an impact on the ability of Icelandic corporates/households/financial companies to honour foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt obligations".  \par \par \uc2 And if foreign lenders withdrew credit lines, "banks could be forced to either demand early repayment from customers or to convert loans into the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (with a substantially higher interest rate)".  \par \par \uc2 "Either scenario could result in higher loan losses," it said.  \par \par \uc2 But Deutsche said that in a worst-case scenario \b \highlight22\uc2 Iceland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with help from other Scandinavian governments, to guarantee senior debt and deposits.  \par \par \uc2 dw FINANCIAL-ICELAND (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|DA|FN|NW|SW|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060407e247001qf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
