\par\par \b \uc2  Iceland crown seen stabilising after drop-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jon Svanur Johansson \par \uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 13:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  REYKJAVIK, March 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and increased demand for fish fuelled by the mad cow disease scare in Europe will help the Icelandic crown stabilise in the next few weeks after months of decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The crown has been on a downward path since early May last year on fears about its longer-term stability caused by the country's high dependence on fish exports and outflows for foreign loan repayments and swap closures. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Iceland central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on four occasions in January and February in support of the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 1030 GMT on Monday the crown was at 121.8 points on the inverted trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  index , which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses to set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band. The band's lower limit, or weakest point is 125.36 and the upper limit at 104.66. The crown was trading around 108 at the beginning of May 2000. \par \par \uc2  "So far the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measures have been successful and now we are even starting to experience a seasonal cash inflow, such as from fisheries," said Bjarni Kristjansson, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at National Bank of Iceland (Landsbanki). \par \par \uc2  Asked if rising fish exports were also a result of the European mad-cow disease scare, Kristjansson said: "This is definitely a factor." \par \par \uc2  Consumers across Europe have been increasing their consumption of fish in the wake of the mad cow crisis which has seen beef sales slump. Fish accounts for around 70 percent of Iceland's exports. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still lose some ground in the near term but should not breach its trading band. \par \par \uc2  "For now there is no reason to believe that the crown is in any danger. I would not be surprised if it continued to weaken a little bit but in the long run I don't believe that it will exceed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band limits," said Sigurgeir Jonsson, head of derivatives trading at investment bank Kaupthing. \par \par \uc2  RATE CUT SEEN \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may boost market confidence in the crown by cutting interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "I would not be surprised if they were to lower the rates in the next few weeks," Jonsson said. \par \par \uc2  "By doing that they would send a certain signal into the market... they were determined to prevent an economic downswing. Were they to send this message we would have every reason to be optimistic," he said. \par \par \uc2  After several rises last year the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repo rate now stands at 11.4 percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last month that it was not yet time to loosen monetary policy, but that it would consider a cut if there was more evidence of an economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3c045ji\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
